# Global-scope secret defaults — template. Copy to secrets-global.conf.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# Bitwarden Secrets Manager holds one secret per account/collaborator, so a
# single env name legitimately maps to several BWS keys:
#
#     OPENAI_API_KEY - work
#     OPENAI_API_KEY - personal
#
# Per-repo callers are unaffected: `setup-envrc` writes the exact
# "ENV - description" key into that repo's .envrc, so it is pinned by
# construction. The GLOBAL scope — Claude Code hooks, wrappers, anything
# resolving a bare env name — has no such pin. This file is where that scope
# declares which one it means.
#
# Without a declaration, an ambiguous name is a hard error rather than a silent
# empty result. That matters: the silent version cost ~349 auto-mode denials
# before anyone noticed the key was never being injected.
#
# FORMAT
#     ENV_NAME = <exact BWS key>
# Split on the first "=", surrounding whitespace trimmed; "#" comments and blank
# lines ignored. Keys containing " - " and ":" are fine. Only consulted when a
# name is ACTUALLY ambiguous, so adding a new account's key needs no edit here
# unless it should become the global default.
#
# ORDERED PREFERENCE + BLOCKED STATE
# Repeat an env name to declare a preference list. Resolution returns the first
# entry that is not blocked. Prefix a value with "!" to mark it blocked:
#
#     RUNPOD_API_KEY = !RUNPOD_API_KEY - lin.yulong
#     RUNPOD_API_KEY = RUNPOD_API_KEY - Phuong_c8:Yulong
# (the first entry is blocked and skipped; the second resolves — comments
# are whole-line only, an inline "#" would be read as part of the key)
#
# Resolution NEVER auto-switches: a 401 on the active key does not promote the
# next one, because failing over would silently start spending from an account
# you did not choose. Blocking is a human action (`secrets-use ENV --next`).
# If every declared key is blocked, resolution hard-errors rather than falling
# back — the same fail-loud contract as an undeclared ambiguous name.
#
# A single-line declaration remains valid and is the common case.
#
# THE "[global]" NAME MARKER
# A literal " [global]" suffix on the NAME field, before the "=", declares that
# this env name may be resolved by bare name outside a repo — the machine's
# deliberate exception for Claude Code hooks and direnv, which have no terminal:
#
#     ANTHROPIC_API_KEY [global] = ANTHROPIC_API_KEY - mats
#     OPENAI_API_KEY = OPENAI_API_KEY - tomek
#     RUNPOD_API_KEY [global] = !RUNPOD_API_KEY - lin.yulong
#
# The marker attaches to the NAME; the "!" block prefix attaches to a VALUE.
# They are orthogonal, so a blocked line keeps its name marker — blocking is
# per-key, exposure is per-name. A name counts as global if ANY of its lines
# carries the marker, so a hand-edited, partially-tagged file still behaves
# predictably. Because exposure and account choice are separate axes, switching
# which key is active never changes whether the name is globally resolvable.
#
# A marker line with no value (`HF_TOKEN [global] =`) grants the exposure
# without pinning an account — what an unambiguous name needs, and what lets the
# marker be written before BWS is even installed.
#
# A `# global-scope-decided: NAME` comment records that this machine has already
# decided that name's global exposure — either deploy's one-time migration or an
# explicit `secrets-use NAME --no-global`. Deploy stands down when it sees one,
# so a revocation is not undone by the next deployment. It records the DECISION,
# not the marking: revoking a name that was never marked changes no declaration
# line, and that is exactly the case where the record matters. Delete the line
# to let deploy decide the name again.
#
# Every mutation takes a `secrets-global.conf.lock` directory for the whole
# read-decide-rewrite, so a deploy running while you switch accounts cannot land
# an outcome equal to neither ordering. A stale lock after a killed writer is
# safe to `rmdir` by hand. `SECRETS_USE_TEST_PRE_MV_DELAY` is a test-only seam
# used to force that interleaving in the suite — never set it in normal use.
#
# EDIT VIA `secrets-use`, NOT BY HAND
#     secrets-use                          # interactive picker
#     secrets-use ENV_NAME <key-or-desc>   # set the active key
#     secrets-use ENV_NAME --next          # block current, promote next
#     secrets-use ENV_NAME --activate <k>  # unblock (reverses --next)
#     secrets-use ENV_NAME --global        # allow bare-name use outside a repo
#     secrets-use ENV_NAME --no-global     # revoke that (the default state)
#     secrets-use --list                   # show state, order, blocked keys
#
# Discover the exact keys with:
#     dotfiles-secrets keys-meta      # env_name<TAB>bws_key<TAB>note
#
# The real secrets-global.conf is gitignored and stays per-machine: BWS key
# labels name real accounts and collaborators, and this repo is public. Same
# pattern as config/machines.conf.
#
# It is resolved against the MAIN checkout, so every git worktree shares one
# machine-wide answer rather than silently having none.

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY = ANTHROPIC_API_KEY - personal
# OPENAI_API_KEY = OPENAI_API_KEY - personal
